The simple answer is "Yes".

The full answer is not quite that simple, but gets deeper into philosophical
technicalia and the history of ideas than I have time to go into right now. 
Prod me sometime and I'll write an essay on it.

>                           Is Objectivist metaphysics completely or partially
> *wrong*; or is it *incomplete*, but salvagable through amendment?

It's wrong.  The core belief that reality is perfectly describable by
Aristotelian predicates and the "Law of Identity" makes it unsalvageable.
That belief, an essentially *metaphysical* assertion, poisons the epistemology
erected on top of it.

The final, crushing irony is that Rand could have avoided this error and still
built a nearly identical praxis if her knowledge of technical philosophy
hadn't stopped at about 1850.

