"Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com> wrote:
>No one has yet given me even the remotest indication of what is wrong
>with objectivism.
[deleted]
>Could someone say precisely what is wrong with objectivism, other than
>the crappy epsistemology?

    There has been a lot of confusion on the list recently about
Objectivism and objectivism. Most of the problem has come from the
fact that we are using the same words to indicate _very_ different
things, and not explaining our usage so the others can understand our
meaning. Since "little o" objectivism and "big O" Objectivism has
become the list's convention, I will use it. (Also it's less biased
than the terms I usually use, "real objectivism" and "rational
objectivism.") The definitions I will be using are:

Big O Objectivism: Objectivism (TM) as practiced by Ayn Rand, her
personal disciples, and the fanatic level groups who would be
considered acceptable as real Objectivists by the group she personally
led (inherited by Piekoff on her death).

Little o objectivism: Objectivism as practiced by people who read a
few of her books and adopted part of her philosophy without
internalizing the intolerance, irrationallity and self-certainty.
(alt.objectivism ;-)


> Don't include things like "Objectivists
>smoke" or "Ayn Rand was a jerk who thought that her opinions on music
>were objectively true" either, since neither smoking nor Ayn Rand's
>personal opinions on art have anything really to do with the core of
>objectivism.

    This strikes to the heart of the problem between Perry (and other
little o objectivists who have never dealt with a group of big O
Objectivists) and Ed (and other former big O Objectivists). Big O
Objectivists aren't allowed to have opinions that contradict Rand's
writings. 
    It says in chapter 5,000,000,032 of Atlas Shrugged that smoking is
a good thing, and Ayn Rand chain-smoked, so an Objectivist who doesn't
smoke is encouraged to start, and one who does is encouraged to
chain-smoke. (You can guess how supportive they are toward a member
who tries to quit.) This is no more open to debate based on the
scientific studies than Creation is in a fundamentalist group. (Less
actually, I know only of only one Baptist excomunication recently, but
many Objectivist excomunications.)
    Also Ayn Rand wrote an article where she "proved" that rock and
roll is evil, and so is everyone who listens to it. Again, this is not
open to debate, except with a newbie who will be inundated with
repetitions of the same arguement.

    There is very little overlap in the mental proccesses taught by
the two groups. Little o objectivists tend to be logical, but big O
Objectivists are no more logical than the worst of the creationists. I
do not believe this last statement is an exageration. It isn't
surprising that rational people often find objectivism attractive and
are drawn into Objectivism. But once they are in, anyone who has a
rational mindset will either leave the group (not nessesarily
voluntarily) or shut down his rational processes. This last option is
what upsets me (and probably Ed Lane) the most. If an
asshole^H^H^H^H^H^H^H personality-challenged individual who can't get
along with anyone, think for himself, or succeed at anything needs
someplace to fit in, where the people around him will tell him he is
better than all those others out there with good jobs, then I would
rather see him go to a big O Objectivist discussion group than to a
Branch Davidian bible study.  (At least they won't wind up voting to
censor biology texts in schools.)
    But the problem comes with people who are capable of doing better.
Like all cults, big O Objectivism tends to catch people at vulnerable
points in their lives, and then the members make themselves the focus
of the new members social life. Once in, the member has a choice to
make, stop thinking, leave, or be kicked out (which includes
ostracism). Since he already tends to think along little o objectivist
line (or he wouldn't have been interested anyway), and the group and
it's members has become very important to him (often they are all
that's left of his social life), the preasure is strong to stop
thinking "evil" thoughts. The only way to do this, is to learn to stop
thinking rationally.
    By contrast, little o objectivist groups seem to enjoy argueing
about everything, which probably builds up the ability to think
logically. (At least they learn to build logical sounding arguments.)
    Instead of discussing issues, big O Objectivists are encouraged to
catagorise anyone who disagrees as either totally evil or good people
deluded by the propoganda of the evil ones. Either way, their
arguments are unimportant, the only reason to listen at all is to find
out what the subject is. If they are deluded, just spout Rand at them
until they see the error of their ways. If they don't accept your
perfectly logical arguments quickly, they must be evil.


    The Law of the Excluded Middle is a big part of big O Objectivism.
People who are Objectivists (or who would become one if it was
explained to them) are good. A=A. Everyone else is not good, and
therefore are totally evil. ~A != A. There is _no_ middle ground.
Anyone who is not good is as evil as Stalin or Hitler. ~A = ~A. The
only difference between Hitler and your next-door neighbor who voted
for Clinton is Hitler got the chance to act. (Can you say "paranoid"?
I thought you could. :-)

a list member who appears to be a big O Objectivist wrote:
> It's the idea that counts. It reads: "We want to kill you. If we can't
> kill you, we'll make we control your every move and make your life
> miserable."
[and in another post, same subject.]
> I do not have time to do that research
>for Lefty. I suggest he does it himself, as he is apparently involved in
>the environmentalist cult, and should take steps to protect himself
>before it is too late. I just do not have time now to save Lefty from
>the cult now. Sorry.
    This is typical of what Objectivists are taught. He was talking
about environmentalists. _Not_ the extremists in the movement, not
some environmentalists, not even _most_ environmentalists. All
environmentalists are either good but decieved, or not good. 
    And being an environmentalist is also a boolian variable, _any_
statement that we shouldn't totally destroy the Earth makes you as
much of an environmentalist as the worst fanatic. 
    Many big O Objectivists started out this way. Others,
unfortunatly, started out mainly rational and _learn_ to think like
this. 


    This type of abuse of Aristotelian logic isn't limited to
Objectivists, of course. (Satan, for example, _wants_ to do evil. And
Dan Davis pointed out to me in email that Captain Planet has bad guys
who go around polluting for the sake of destruction.) Most fanatics
think anyone who disagrees at all about their core issue is a fanatic
for the other side. But I can't think of any other group that applies
this illogic to everything, and can only think of a few groups who
explicitly teach it to their members about their opponants.
    Environmentalists implicitly teach (by acusations about specific
individuals) that their enemies want to destroy the Earth, but can
anyone think of an example where they sit down and teach it as a
logical arguement, with everything spelled out for all to see?


